<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The sandbox, and the one grant it needs.

  One file for three bundles, because nothing in it is per-application: each
  opens the file a person chose and saves back to it or to another file the
  same panel names.

  **The entitlement does nothing until it is inside a signature.** An unsigned
  bundle is not sandboxed, so every measurement of sandbox behaviour has to be
  made against a signed one, and an Apple Development certificate is enough for
  that. This is why `build-app.sh` signs even for a development build, and why
  nothing about the sandbox can be settled from Linux.

  `files.user-selected.read-write`, because these applications edit and save.
  Read-only was correct before the editing branch landed and is not now: a
  save under a read-only grant is refused by the sandbox itself, silently to
  the application and as a permission error to nobody, since there is no
  window to report it in. A capability asked for and unused is a question at
  review with no good answer - which is the read-only grant's own argument for
  itself, and exactly why it has to move the day the capability stops being
  unused.

  Nothing else. No network, no broad filesystem access, no device, and no
  keychain group even though the provisioning profile grants one.

  Author: David M. Anderson
  Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
